css='''body{margin:0;background:#151116;color:#e9dfca;font:17px/1.65 system-ui}main{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:28px}h1,h2,h3{color:#e2bf85;line-height:1.22}h1{font-size:38px}h2{margin-top:45px}a{color:#e5b77f}nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px}.note{padding:20px;border-left:3px solid #b68c52;background:#251b24}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px}td,th{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #584451}img,video{max-width:100%;background:#0d0b11}.pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px}.pair img{width:100%}.pair>*{min-width:0}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}code{overflow-wrap:anywhere}details{margin:18px 0}li{margin:8px 0}@media(max-width:650px){main{padding:16px}.pair{grid-template-columns:1fr}h1{font-size:28px}.scroll{overflow:auto}.scroll table{min-width:660px}}'''
body='''<p>Dreamscapes · Cycle Five · 7 November 2026 · local creative review</p><h1>Let the object make the invitation</h1><nav><a href="/carnival/?scene=fortune-teller&cycle-five=zora">Play Zora’s table</a><a href="/carnival/?scene=fortune-teller&cycle-five=zora&observations=1">Play with evidence inspector</a><a href="http://127.0.0.1:4255/review/">Cycle Four comparison</a><a href="http://127.0.0.1:4245/carnival/?scene=rides-games">Approved carousel</a><a href="#recordings">Recordings</a></nav>
<p class="note"><strong>Finding:</strong> object interaction can replace the questionnaire-shaped interface, but it does not automatically make the resulting taste evidence stronger. The strongest next step is a short test of whether players notice, understand and intentionally pursue these invitations—before connecting them to recommendations.</p>
<h2>1. Independent critique of Cycle Four</h2><p>Cycle Four did useful work separating raw observations from uncertain book inference. Its local proxy, isolated store, complete offer receipt and duplicate handling are worth retaining. Its creative weakness is substantial: a large panel explains the experience, the player selects a summary, and another paragraph tells them what happened. The payoff has no physical existence in the scene.</p><p>The earlier roadmap correctly resisted twenty new quests and premature match percentages. I would change its order: do not commit to a larger Zora story loop yet. First establish that players can discover the invitation and recognize the difference between examining an object and pursuing its story. The visual version below is a testable mechanism, not proof that Zora is now the best long-term hub or that these two premises cover realistic or character-focused readers.</p>
<h2>2. Working design: two tickets, one table</h2><p>A loose ticket rests on the existing painted table. Examining it brings the player closer. One ticket lies flat; another balances on edge. Lifting the flat ticket exposes a blank reverse. Holding it in the lamp reveals the serial 07 · 24 · 13; a second matching ticket slips from the cloth. The upright ticket swings open to a moonlit view that cannot fit inside a sheet of paper.</p><p>Examining either object is reversible. Taking the second ticket chooses the hidden-message direction and leaves a trail of tickets toward the curtain. Entering the opening chooses the impossible-door direction; the view expands and the first ticket is left beyond the threshold. The resulting discovery survives closing and reloading within the same session. The player can leave without choosing.</p><p>No premise paragraphs, visible instruction panel, confirmation menu, points or reward advantage appear. Only existing printed artwork, a short in-world serial and the exit glyph are visible. Screen readers receive concise descriptions of the same events and named controls. Keyboard Enter/Space works; held-key repeats are suppressed. Touch uses ordinary taps, avoiding a compulsory drag or dexterity test. Reduced motion replaces movement with the equivalent final states.</p><p>I deliberately used a second ticket instead of a moth: it reuses approved art, gives the two invitations a shared physical vocabulary and avoids needing an unapproved character animation. The existing Zora painting, loose-ticket image and Griffin citadel painting are referenced unchanged. Table, lamp and opening are lightweight CSS prototype shapes—not new generated artwork. Their finish needs creative review; no permanent replacement art was introduced.</p>
<div class="pair"><figure><img src="adults-desktop-comparison.jpg" alt="Both investigated objects on Zora’s table"><figcaption>Desktop: investigation before pursuit.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="adults-phone-consequence.jpg" alt="Phone-sized impossible opening consequence"><figcaption>Phone emulation: the chosen opening changes the view.</figcaption></figure></div>
<p><strong>Creative limits:</strong> the ticket trail is a visible consequence, not a complete mystery with a solved identity. The citadel is a glimpse, not a navigable new city. The doorway is more spectacular than the mystery payoff; that is a salience confound, not evidence for fantasy. The table and lamp are intentionally provisional. There is no physical handset, screen-reader-user or real-player validation yet.</p>
<h2>3. What the observations mean—and do not mean</h2><div class="scroll"><table><tr><th>Action</th><th>Available alternatives</th><th>Interpretation</th><th>Limits</th></tr><tr><td>Examine table; lift a ticket</td><td>Both physical objects; step back</td><td>Exploration only</td><td>May be accidental, visually attracted or learning controls.</td></tr><tr><td>Reveal marks / open paper view</td><td>Other object, available pursuit action, leave</td><td>Discovery only</td><td>Completing the mechanic is not a genre vote or intelligence score.</td></tr><tr><td>Take second ticket</td><td>Only pursuit actions actually unlocked plus examine controls and leave</td><td>Observed pursuit of a mystery; tentative investigative-story hypothesis only if both were examined and available</td><td>Numbers, texture, order, novelty or seeking completion may explain it.</td></tr><tr><td>Enter paper opening</td><td>Only pursuit actions actually unlocked plus examine controls and leave</td><td>Observed pursuit of an impossible event; tentative speculative-story hypothesis only with comparison</td><td>Animation and scenery are strong confounds. Not a validated fantasy-book preference.</td></tr><tr><td>Leave, return or reload</td><td>Retreat remains available</td><td>Behavior, with the consequence retained</td><td>No dislike; no extra independent evidence.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>All new records use the existing observation core’s <em>exploration</em> and <em>gameplay</em> categories, with no preference attributes. Raw records include action, actual enabled alternatives, order, age context, motion mode and whether both objects had been examined. A separate pure interpretation function emits qualitative hypotheses only. Unlike Cycle Four’s catalog-supported “explicit preference,” no gameplay action is presented as a direct book preference. Test fixtures carry <code>origin: test_fixture</code> and are excluded.</p><p>Each age context can have one pursuit record for this version in the local session. Interpretation considers only the current age context; changing age does not carry an older hypothesis across. Reopening or reloading does not create another. The two discovery clicks plus pursuit are one episode—not three independent signals. A one-sided pursuit is retained as raw action but classified as insufficient comparison. No unchosen option becomes negative evidence. Session storage can fall back to memory; cross-session persistence and cross-device identity are not implemented.</p>
<h2>4. Text choices versus gameplay evidence</h2><div class="scroll"><table><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Cycle Four</th><th>Cycle Five</th></tr><tr><td>Alternatives understood</td><td>Explicit full premises; wording/framing bias.</td><td>Concrete actions but story meaning is less certain. Availability is not proof of comprehension.</td></tr><tr><td>Accidental selection</td><td>One button chooses immediately.</td><td>Inspection is separated from pursuit. Still vulnerable to clicking every active object; no claim it is eliminated.</td></tr><tr><td>World consequence</td><td>Prose tells an ending.</td><td>Objects and view visibly change and remain on return.</td></tr><tr><td>Inference</td><td>Direct interest in a written premise; tentative transfer to books.</td><td>Direct observed action, tentative narrative interest; transfer remains unvalidated and may be weaker.</td></tr><tr><td>Independent evidence</td><td>One invitation decision.</td><td>Still one episode. More event rows do not mean stronger independent evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>Readable premise with conventional controls.</td><td>Native named buttons, focus, status descriptions, reversible examination and reduced-motion states; real assistive-technology usability unverified.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Synthetic test paths demonstrate different records: desktop fixtures investigate both then take the ticket; phone fixtures investigate both then enter the opening. Unit scenarios also cover one-sided exploration, declining, duplicates, reload, synthetic exclusion and age change. These are controlled software checks, not synthetic proof of reader preference. <a href="synthetic-comparison.json">Labelled path-by-path raw observation comparison</a>.</p>
<h2>5. An interaction language for the wider game</h2><p>Use three consistent verbs: <strong>examine</strong> brings an object closer without committing; <strong>use / approach</strong> produces a visible response; <strong>pursue</strong> crosses a clearly changed threshold or takes a newly revealed object. A fourth reliable action, <strong>step back</strong>, must always work. Do not add a survey-style confirmation to compensate for unclear visual staging.</p><p>At rest, use framing, contrast and occasional restrained world motion. Hover and keyboard focus should share a subtle material response; focus must remain visible even if less “cinematic.” Touch gets equivalent tap-to-examine behavior, never hover-only discovery. A newly revealed object should appear where its cause makes sense. No carpet of floating icons, permanent glow rings or arrows. Keep destination names and outcome descriptions in accessible labels/live status; preserve necessary visible rules, scores, age selection and error messages.</p>'''
body+='<div class="scroll"><table><tr><th>Scene</th><th>Current written UI</th><th>Proposed treatment</th><th>Status</th></tr>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(x)+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in inventory)+'</table></div>'
body+='''<h2>6. Recommendation research and real-reader validation</h2><p>The current NovelIdeas sources were rechecked read-only; all 17 inspected Cycle Four excerpts remain present. V1 remains the default deck, with eight Taste DNA axes. V2 uses positive/avoid signals in genre family, tone, themes, character dynamics, pacing and format. Its swipe adapter accepts like/dislike/skip; its minimum weighting and age-specific skip semantics make fake low-weight game “swipes” unsafe. The Cycle Four integration conclusion still holds: use a separate provenance-aware adapter, never pretend a ticket is a book rating.</p><p>The present episode might eventually inform mystery/investigative or surreal/speculative interest. It does not justify updating warmth, darkness, humor, complexity, character focus, pacing or idea density. The painted citadel alone cannot determine realism preference. Strong explicit book evidence must prevail. Keep conflicting positive interests as alternatives, and hold conflicts with explicit avoids for review rather than cancelling or reversing established preferences. Later genuinely different story decisions may support or weaken a hypothesis; clicking the same motif again does not.</p><p>For example, a player who enters the paper opening and later pursues a grounded investigation may enjoy both kinds of story; the later choice is not a dislike of fantasy. A retrospective explanation such as “I only wanted to see the animation” weakens the literary interpretation without deleting the observed action. Investigative choices across genuinely different contexts could support a hypothesis, but repeated ticket clicks cannot. Trusted student eligibility must come from the authenticated recommendation context, not the game’s visual age selector. Preserve publication identity, source, maturity, diversity and final eligibility gates. No recommender source, scores, network recommendation calls or counter were changed. <a href="recommendation-audit.json">Current source hashes and prior-excerpt checks</a>.</p><p><strong>Next reader test:</strong> begin with 12–16 consenting readers as a formative usability pilot, not a statistically powered prediction study, across supported age contexts, with appropriate guardian/school arrangements for younger participants. Observe silently whether they discover both objects and can later describe what pursuit meant. Let them pass. Counterbalance side, order and visual emphasis; do not equate the more animated route with a preferred genre. Ask brief retrospective reasons after play, not during it.</p><p>Then compare a counterbalanced text version and object version using a fixed, age-eligible set of independently labelled book blurbs, shuffled without revealing the inferred genre. Record voluntary “would try” interest and explicit prior interests separately. Compare against explicit-interest-only and mount-only baselines on held-out people, not repeated clicks from the same person. Predefine success metrics, exclusions and the primary outcome; determine a confirmatory sample size from pilot variability rather than inventing confidence percentages. Report uncertainty and subgroup usability. This can validate incremental prediction of blurb interest; it cannot establish enjoyment of a whole book.</p><p>Stop or revise if readers cannot explain the alternatives, if side/salience dominates, if passing feels like failure, or if the scene does not entertain without recommendations. Good gameplay may still be worth retaining even if predictive lift is zero.</p>
<h2 id="performance">7. Performance investigation</h2><div id="performance-data"></div>
<p>Corrected desktop trials measured 23–26 fps with approved painting and 29–32 fps with hidden SVG painting skipped. Phone-sized trials measured 31–32 versus 57–60 fps on the same computer. Cycle Four used a longer 5–35 second window; these 7–19 second windows are not a direct across-cycle benchmark. These are fresh results, not a reproduction of an exact 16–18 fps ceiling; they reinforce that capture is not required for live slowdown. The diagnostic intercepts the local browser’s versioned carousel script and skips external SVG painting only during its measurement window, <em>after</em> publishing the authoritative phase/elapsed clock. No public source is changed. Fresh contexts prevent resuming old rides. A/B/B/A conditions use the same Dragon, adults style and approximate 7–19 second live window at each viewport. No video capture runs during these comparisons. Each receipt checks that the interception executed, time advanced normally and the window began on a fresh ride.</p><p>A receipt from discarded harness work is retained separately: the first missed the script’s query string; another resumed saved state after unload re-saved an interrupted ride. Neither is evidence of an optimization effect. Even the corrected comparisons are small, sequential and share the host with normal applications; thermal/load and camera/phase differences remain limitations. They do not establish a guaranteed percentage improvement.</p><div id="profile-data"></div>
<div class="scroll"><table><tr><th>Candidate</th><th>Evidence / likely benefit</th><th>Risk and visual impact</th></tr><tr><td>Separate authoritative ride clock from hidden external SVG painting</td><td>Directly tested in isolation; see measured results. First optimization candidate if benefit repeats.</td><td>Moderate integration risk: restore current external pose before return; test boarding, interruption, age/style change and reduced motion. No intended visible change; not promoted this cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>GPU / shared renderer and canvas transfer</td><td>Fresh timed WebGL trace plus shared renderer source; rendering is material. The canvas bridge and repeated resize are candidates, not proven exclusive causes.</td><td>Medium/high risk, especially iOS SVG holders and compositing. First profile the individual stages; do not rewrite the renderer on suspicion.</td></tr><tr><td>Geometry / visible model count</td><td>Cycle Four reported roughly 699k triangles / 35 calls per ride render. GPU cost is measured, but geometry versus shading/texture cost is not causally separated.</td><td>Try verified invisible-object culling in an isolated experiment, with pixel comparisons; no automatic decimation or mount removal.</td></tr><tr><td>Textures and dream cross-fades</td><td>Paintings preload before ride; opacity changes on a cylinder. No isolated evidence that the transition is the dominant cause.</td><td>Measure phase-specific GPU cost first. Do not shrink or replace approved paintings, remove the fade, or alter timing.</td></tr><tr><td>Resolution / device</td><td>Source caps ride dimensions at 1280 by 1000; actual renderer/device receipt is recorded. Phone viewport is the same computer.</td><td>No resolution downgrade. Test target physical devices and browsers before setting a performance target.</td></tr></table></div>
<h2 id="recordings">8. Ordinary-speed recordings and verification</h2><p>Full automated interaction recordings, without frame acceleration, trimming or retiming. These demonstrate controls and consequences, not human enjoyment. Viewport emulation is not physical-device testing.</p><div class="pair"><div><h3>Desktop · hidden message</h3><video controls preload="metadata" src="artifacts/adults-desktop.webm"></video></div><div><h3>Phone-sized · impossible opening</h3><video controls preload="metadata" src="artifacts/adults-phone.webm"></video></div></div><div id="test-data"></div>
<h2>9. Prioritized Cycle Six proposal</h2><ol><li><strong>First, creative usability review.</strong> Test object discoverability, clarity of pursuit and comparative appeal with real readers. Balance the mystery payoff against the spectacular opening. Commission a small matching table/lamp/ticket-back treatment only if the mechanism survives review; do not generate a whole new room.</li><li><strong>Finish one consequence.</strong> Let a kept ticket affect one existing later scene—ideally a modest Forest’s Edge or mirror event with a genuine follow-up decision. Deliver a beginning, consequence and resolution before adding more invitations. Do not mistake replay or a mandatory clue for independent preference evidence.</li><li><strong>Performance stabilization.</strong> Validate the hidden-SVG candidate with exact-pose image comparisons, full return transitions, all carousel styles and target devices before any production change. Re-profile GPU cost afterward.</li><li><strong>Only then, shadow recommendation validation.</strong> Preserve raw decisions and exposure receipts; compare against explicit interests and held-out blurb choices with trusted age gating. No percentage counter until its meaning and coverage are defensible.</li></ol><p>Technical readiness: an isolated visual interaction, local persistence, raw evidence and regression infrastructure exist. Creative readiness: the event has not been validated with players and its two outcomes are not equally compelling yet. Recommendation readiness: hypotheses only; no demonstrated predictive lift.</p>
<h2>10. Preservation and reproducibility</h2><p>Base <code>49893f5e</code>; branch <code>codex/dreamscapes-cycle-five</code>. All 20 approved mounts and 17 painting files, their mappings, camera, mechanical selector, early transitions on both revolutions, normal observation framework and normal navigation remain unchanged. Research files stay outside <code>public/</code>, and the server injects them only with <code>cycle-five=zora</code>. The normal 4245 preview remains available.</p><p><a href="baseline.json">Baseline and approved-file hashes</a> · <a href="preservation.json">Final preservation checks</a> · <a href="prototype-results.json">Raw synthetic browser receipts</a> · <a href="reproduce.txt">Reproduction steps and limits</a></p><p>No push, merge, deployment, publication, recommendation-counter implementation or automatic next cycle.</p>'''
# Fill measured data only after the files exist.
perf=json.loads((out/'performance-results.json').read_text()) if (out/'performance-results.json').exists() else []
perfhtml='<p><a href="performance-results.json">Corrected live comparison receipts</a></p><table><tr><th>Viewport</th><th>Condition</th><th>FPS</th><th>Window seconds</th></tr>'+''.join(f'<tr><td>{"phone emulation" if r["mobile"] else "desktop"}</td><td>{"skip hidden SVG (test only)" if r["skipHiddenSVG"] else "approved painting"}</td><td>{r["fps"]:.1f}</td><td>{r["start"]/1000:.1f}–{r["end"]/1000:.1f}</td></tr>' for r in perf)+'</table>'
body=body.replace('<div id="performance-data"></div>',perfhtml)
profile=out/'performance/profile.json'
if profile.exists():
 p=json.loads(profile.read_text());g=p['gpu']['summary'];t=p['mainThreadRegions'];pro=f'<p>Fresh separate profiled desktop run: {p["profiledFPS"]:.1f} fps; WebGL region median {g["medianMs"]:.1f} ms, p95 {g["p95Ms"]:.1f} ms across {g["samples"]} valid queries. Profiling adds overhead; do not substitute this FPS for the comparison. Main-thread regions nest and overlap: do not add them as exclusive shares. <a href="performance/profile.json">Profile receipt</a> · <a href="performance/runtime.json">GPU/runtime receipt</a>.</p>'
else:pro='<p>Fresh profile pending; do not infer missing results.</p>'
body=body.replace('<div id="profile-data"></div>',pro)
tests=[]
for file,label in [('prototype-results.json','prototype age/layout cases'),('observations/results.json','existing observation browser cases'),('edge-results.json','prototype resilience cases'),('wheel-ready/results.json','existing wheel lifecycle cases')]:
 if (out/file).exists():
  rows=json.loads((out/file).read_text());rows=rows.get('results',[]) if isinstance(rows,dict) else rows;tests.append(f'<li>{sum(bool(x.get("passed")) for x in rows)}/{len(rows)} {label}: <a href="{file}">receipts</a>.</li>')
reg=Path('playtest-artifacts/cycle-five/regression/results.json')
if reg.exists():
 rows=json.loads(reg.read_text())['results'];tests.append(f'<li>{sum(bool(x.get("passed")) for x in rows)}/{len(rows)} existing Carnival regression cases: <a href="artifacts/regression/report.html">full report</a>.</li>')
body=body.replace('<div id="test-data"></div>','<ul><li>60 core/prototype/existing-server tests plus six Cycle Five server tests pass: <a href="unit-tests.txt">core log</a> · <a href="server-tests.txt">new server log</a>.</li>'+''.join(tests)+'</ul><p>New raw-action tests explicitly cover synthetic exclusion, no inference from discovery, no unchosen dislikes, insufficient comparison, age separation and reload deduplication. Accessibility testing is programmatic keyboard/touch/reduced-motion coverage, not an audit with assistive-technology users.</p>')
(out/'index.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Dreamscapes Cycle Five creative review</title><style>'+css+'</style><main>'+body+'</main></html>',encoding='utf-8')
for name in ['adults-desktop','adults-phone']:
 (out/f'ride-{name}.html').write_text(f'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{name} Cycle Five playback</title><body style="margin:0;background:#110d15;color:#ead8bb"><video style="width:100%;height:95vh" controls preload="metadata" src="artifacts/{name}.webm"></video></body></html>')
